ML_PAGE("t2-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l2\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">TOPIC 2 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain the mechanism, apply it to the case, evaluate the trade-off, recommend. The answer technique and model answers are below.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l2\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>The 5-move ERM answer, tuned for Topic 2</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. Define</b><p>ERM, a business form, a risk level or an RM objective, in the slide's terms.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Explain why</b><p>Usually \"why the old approach became insufficient\" or \"who bears the loss\".</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. Apply</b><p>Place the case's facts at the right level: strategic, tactical, operational, compliance.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. Evaluate</b><p>Silo vs enterprise view; risk vs expected value; costs of each structure.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>5. Recommend</b><p>A justified structure, treatment or governance step.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"card-note\">Topic 2's strongest answers show interaction: one risk cannot be understood independently of other risks or of strategy.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box span-all\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p>\"ERM is better than traditional RM because it is holistic.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">A slogan: no mechanism, no case, no condition.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"Traditional RM may remain efficient for narrow, well-defined exposures, but as the manufacturer expands internationally its FX, supplier and debt risks become dependent on the same markets. Managed in silos, each appears acceptable while their combined exposure could threaten survival. ERM aggregates these risks against the firm's objectives, so it is more suitable here.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-good\">Condition + mechanism (correlation) + case + conclusion.</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n<h3 class=\"block-title\">Model-answer bank</h3>\n<div class=\"scenario-grid\">\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Explain unlimited liability and its risk implication for a sole proprietor. (5 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t2-1\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t2-1\">Unlimited liability means the owner is personally liable for all business debts and obligations, so business losses can reach personal assets: if a business with RM100,000 of assets owes RM500,000, creditors may pursue the owner's savings and property. Because one person also provides the capital, control and expertise, the business has a single point of failure and little resilience. The structure's simplicity reduces administrative complexity but concentrates risk on the owner.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">COMPARE</span>\n        <h4>Compare strategic, tactical, operational and compliance risk using one company. (8 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t2-2\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t2-2\">For a carmaker going EV-first: strategic risk is whether the direction succeeds (will customers buy EVs?); tactical risk is how it is executed (financing, battery suppliers, pricing, marketing, IT); operational risk is daily execution (machine failure, human error, supply interruption); compliance risk is whether it is permitted (battery safety and environmental rules). The levels interact: a compliance failure can halt operations and undermine the strategy, so they must be managed as one system.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Explain the pre-loss and post-loss objectives of risk management. (8 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t2-3\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t2-3\">Pre-loss objectives prepare the firm before a loss: prepare for potential losses in the most economical way, reduce anxiety, and meet legal obligations (e.g. sprinklers, training and insurance). Post-loss objectives apply after a loss: ensure survival, continue operations, stabilise earnings, maintain growth, and minimise the effects on people and society (e.g. relocating production after a fire and paying staff). Risk management is therefore about resilience and recovery, not only prevention.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">ANALYSE</span>\n        <h4>Explain how corporate risk management evolved into ERM and holistic RM. (10 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t2-4\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t2-4\">Risk management began as insurance-based treatment of pure risks such as fire and flood, but insurance cannot cover poor strategy or reputation. Traditional RM (early 20th century) managed risks reactively in departmental silos, and conventional CRM focused on credit, market and liquidity risk using hedging. Silos miss correlated risks, so integrated RM (1980s-90s) linked risk to strategic planning and added operational, reputational and strategic risk. ERM (2000s) is a value-based, holistic framework managing all material risks together for long-term firm value. Holistic RM (recent) integrates sustainability, governance and compliance. Each stage arose because the previous one became insufficient as organisations grew more complex and interconnected.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EVALUATE</span>\n        <h4>A debt-funded acquisition carries IT, supplier and environmental risks. Should the board abandon it? (12 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-t2-5\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-t2-5\">Not necessarily. The objective is market share and shareholder value. Risks: strategic (value may not materialise), financial/liquidity (debt servicing), operational (outdated IT), supplier (single source), compliance/environmental (complaints) and reputational. They interact: a regulator closing the supplier stops production, cuts revenue and raises debt pressure until the strategy fails. Rather than abandoning by reflex, management should treat the risks (supplier diversification, IT integration plan, environmental due diligence, lower leverage, liquidity reserve, governance controls) and proceed only if the residual aggregated risk is acceptable relative to the expected strategic return.</div>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Practice questions from the notes</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P1</span><span>Compare the risk implications of sole proprietorships, partnerships, private limited companies and limited liability... <small class=\"muted\">(10 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Compare the risk implications of sole proprietorships, partnerships, private limited companies and limited liability partnerships. Evaluate why a growing business might change its organisational structure.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>The legal structure of a business affects how ownership, liability, control and continuity risks are distributed among its owners.</p>\n<p>A sole proprietorship is owned and controlled by one individual. Its main advantages include simpler administration, direct decision-making and operational flexibility. However, the owner has unlimited liability and may be personally responsible for business debts. The business may also depend heavily on the owner's capital, managerial capability and continued involvement.</p>\n<p>A general partnership involves two or more owners who may contribute additional financial resources, expertise and management capabilities. Nevertheless, the lecture describes general partners as having unlimited liability, including joint and several responsibility for partnership debts. Partnerships may also experience disagreements, conflicts of interest and difficulties maintaining continuity.</p>\n<p>A private limited company (Sdn. Bhd.) is a separate legal entity from its shareholders. Shareholders generally have limited liability, meaning that their financial exposure is ordinarily limited to their investment. This creates a clearer separation between personal and corporate obligations.</p>\n<p>A limited liability partnership (LLP) combines partnership-style flexibility with limited-liability protection. This structure may be suitable for businesses that want shared management and a degree of legal separation between the firm and its partners.</p>\n<p>For a growing enterprise, changing its legal structure may help reduce personal liability exposure, improve organisational continuity and support additional investment.</p>\n<p>However, limited liability does not eliminate the company's underlying business risks. The company may still face financial losses, operational failures or insolvency.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the choice of business structure should be based on the owners' liability tolerance, financing requirements, management preferences and long-term organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>This comparison follows the liability and legal-entity distinctions presented on pages 4-5 and 16-18.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P2</span><span>Discuss the major objectives of a corporation. <small class=\"muted\">(8 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Discuss the major objectives of a corporation. Explain why maximising short-term profit may conflict with long-term shareholder wealth and sustainability.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Corporations pursue several objectives, including survival, competitiveness, sales growth, profit maximisation, cost minimisation, market-share growth and shareholder-wealth maximisation.</p>\n<p>These objectives are interconnected but may conflict.</p>\n<p>For example, a company may minimise costs by reducing employee training, maintenance expenditure and quality-control activities.</p>\n<p>Although this decision may increase short-term accounting profit, it could also increase the frequency of operational failures, product defects and workplace accidents.</p>\n<p>These events may subsequently create financial losses, regulatory problems and reputational damage, potentially reducing long-term shareholder value.</p>\n<p>Similarly, a company may increase market share by aggressively reducing prices. Although sales volume may increase, profit margins may decline.</p>\n<p>The corporation must therefore evaluate decisions against multiple objectives rather than assuming that maximising one performance indicator automatically improves every other objective.</p>\n<p>The chapter also introduces the Triple Bottom Line, which considers People, Planet and Profit. This broadens the assessment of corporate performance beyond current financial results to include social and environmental considerations.</p>\n<p>Consequently, sound risk management should help companies balance short-term financial objectives with business continuity, competitiveness, stakeholder interests and long-term value creation.</p>\n<p>The lecture identifies these corporate objectives on page 20 and contrasts the single financial bottom line with the Triple Bottom Line on page 21.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P3</span><span>Differentiate between strategic, tactical, operational and compliance risks. <small class=\"muted\">(8 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Differentiate between strategic, tactical, operational and compliance risks. Explain how these risk levels may interact when a company enters a foreign market.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Enterprise risks can be examined at different organisational levels to support risk identification, management and aggregation.</p>\n<p>Strategic risk concerns uncertainty affecting the organisation's long-term direction and ability to achieve its strategic objectives.</p>\n<p>For example, a Malaysian company may decide to enter the Indonesian market. The expansion could fail because management misjudges customer demand or competitive conditions.</p>\n<p>Tactical risk concerns uncertainty affecting plans and decisions used to implement strategic objectives.</p>\n<p>For example, management must determine how to finance the expansion, establish distribution arrangements and implement marketing strategies.</p>\n<p>Operational risk concerns uncertainty affecting day-to-day business activities and the execution of operational plans.</p>\n<p>For example, supply interruptions, system failures or inadequate employee training may prevent the new business from operating efficiently.</p>\n<p>Compliance risk concerns exposure arising from legal and regulatory requirements.</p>\n<p>For example, the company must comply with relevant foreign licensing requirements, labour regulations and business laws.</p>\n<p>Although these risks are classified at different levels, they are interconnected.</p>\n<p>A compliance failure may interrupt operations, increase costs and prevent management from achieving its strategic expansion objective.</p>\n<p>Therefore, examining risks at different levels allows management to identify potential exposures while understanding how failures in one area can affect the entire enterprise.</p>\n<p>This follows the four-level risk diagram on page 23, which distinguishes strategic, tactical, operational and compliance risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P4</span><span>Define risk management and explain how a systematic risk-management process can improve organisational... <small class=\"muted\">(7 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Define risk management and explain how a systematic risk-management process can improve organisational decision-making and resource allocation.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Risk management is a logical and systematic process of identifying, analysing, treating and monitoring risks associated with organisational activities.</p>\n<p>Its purpose is not merely to reduce financial losses but also to improve decision-making, identify opportunities and ensure that available resources are used effectively.</p>\n<p>The process begins with risk identification, where management determines potential events or conditions that may affect business objectives.</p>\n<p>Next, risks are analysed and evaluated to determine their significance and prioritise those requiring management attention.</p>\n<p>Management then selects appropriate risk treatments to reduce or otherwise address relevant exposures.</p>\n<p>Finally, risks and controls are monitored and reviewed to determine whether the responses remain effective as circumstances change.</p>\n<p>A systematic process improves resource allocation because management can direct attention and funds towards risks that could have the greatest impact on organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>For example, a manufacturer facing both frequent minor equipment failures and a potentially catastrophic production interruption should evaluate the relative consequences of these exposures before deciding where to invest in controls.</p>\n<p>Therefore, risk management improves organisational decisions by replacing ad hoc responses with structured analysis, prioritisation, treatment and monitoring.</p>\n<p>The lecture defines risk management as a systematic method and explicitly links it with better decisions, opportunity identification and more effective resource utilisation.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P5</span><span>Differentiate between the pre-loss and post-loss objectives of risk management. <small class=\"muted\">(8 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Differentiate between the pre-loss and post-loss objectives of risk management. Explain why organisations need both.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Risk management has objectives both before and after a loss occurs.</p>\n<p>Pre-loss objectives are concerned with preparing for potential losses in the most economical manner, reducing anxiety associated with uncertain events and complying with relevant legal obligations.</p>\n<p>For example, a manufacturing company may conduct safety training, install fire-protection systems and purchase appropriate insurance before experiencing a major loss.</p>\n<p>These measures help prepare the organisation for potential risk events.</p>\n<p>Post-loss objectives focus on the organisation's ability to survive and recover after a loss occurs.</p>\n<p>They include ensuring business survival, maintaining or restoring operations, stabilising earnings, supporting continued growth and minimising adverse consequences for individuals or society.</p>\n<p>For example, after a fire damages its production facility, a company may activate alternative production arrangements and use available financial resources to maintain essential business functions.</p>\n<p>Both categories are important because preventive preparation cannot guarantee that adverse events will never occur.</p>\n<p>A company may successfully reduce the probability of a fire while still remaining exposed to serious damage if a fire occurs.</p>\n<p>Therefore, effective risk management should balance economical preparation before losses with organisational continuity and recovery after losses.</p>\n<p>These objectives are expressly divided into pre-loss and post-loss categories on page 26.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P6</span><span>Trace the evolution of corporate risk management from traditional insurance-based approaches to Enterprise Risk... <small class=\"muted\">(12 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Trace the evolution of corporate risk management from traditional insurance-based approaches to Enterprise Risk Management. Explain why this evolution became necessary.</p>\n<p>This is one of the highest-priority long-answer questions in Topic 2.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Corporate risk management has evolved from a narrow insurance-based discipline into a more integrated approach that considers a broad range of enterprise risks in relation to long-term organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>First, traditional risk management focused primarily on pure risks and insurance.</p>\n<p>Historically, organisations concentrated on exposures such as fire, theft and natural disasters.</p>\n<p>Insurance was used to transfer or finance some of the financial consequences arising from these events.</p>\n<p>However, this approach placed relatively little emphasis on many uninsurable risks, particularly those arising from business strategy, organisational processes and changing market conditions.</p>\n<p>Second, organisations expanded their focus towards financial risk management.</p>\n<p>As businesses became larger and more complex, management increasingly addressed risks involving credit, liquidity, interest rates and foreign-exchange fluctuations.</p>\n<p>Techniques such as hedging, futures and options became important tools for managing certain financial exposures.</p>\n<p>Nevertheless, risks were often managed through departmental or functional silos.</p>\n<p>For example, a finance department might manage exchange-rate exposure independently while an operations department managed supply-chain risk.</p>\n<p>Third, integrated risk management emerged as organisations recognised that risks could not always be managed independently.</p>\n<p>Organisations began incorporating risk considerations into strategic planning and business decision-making rather than treating risk management exclusively as a reactive function.</p>\n<p>This broader approach included operational, reputational and strategic risks.</p>\n<p>Fourth, Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) developed as a holistic, enterprise-wide approach.</p>\n<p>ERM seeks to identify and manage a wider spectrum of material risks in an integrated manner.</p>\n<p>Instead of evaluating every exposure independently, management considers relationships among risks and their collective implications for corporate objectives.</p>\n<p>For example, geopolitical instability may simultaneously affect suppliers, foreign-exchange costs, customer demand and liquidity.</p>\n<p>Under a silo approach, these risks may appear manageable individually while their combined impact threatens business continuity.</p>\n<p>Finally, the chapter describes a further movement toward holistic risk management.</p>\n<p>This approach increasingly incorporates broader organisational concerns, including sustainability, governance, compliance and emerging technological risks.</p>\n<p>Risk management becomes more closely integrated with the firm's wider management and decision-making activities.</p>\n<p>Overall, this evolution became necessary because modern organisations face increasingly complex and interconnected risks that cannot be adequately addressed through insurance or isolated departmental controls alone.</p>\n<p>Consequently, ERM provides a broader approach to managing uncertainty while supporting strategic objectives and long-term enterprise value.</p>\n<p>The lecture presents this evolution across pages 27-32, with ERM explicitly characterised as a value-based, holistic management approach.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P7</span><span>Critically evaluate the limitations of the traditional silo approach to corporate risk management. <small class=\"muted\">(10 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Critically evaluate the limitations of the traditional silo approach to corporate risk management. Explain how ERM addresses these limitations.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>The silo approach refers to managing risks separately within different functional departments or business units.</p>\n<p>For example, the finance department may manage credit and liquidity risks, the IT department may manage technological risks, and the operations department may manage production-related risks.</p>\n<p>This approach provides certain advantages because specialist departments possess technical expertise and detailed knowledge of their respective exposures.</p>\n<p>However, silo risk management has several limitations.</p>\n<p>First, it may fail to identify risk interdependencies.</p>\n<p>Risks originating in different departments may share common underlying drivers.</p>\n<p>For example, geopolitical instability could simultaneously increase foreign-exchange costs, interrupt suppliers and reduce customer demand.</p>\n<p>Although each department may assess its risk independently, the collective exposure could be substantially greater than expected.</p>\n<p>Second, silo management may prevent management from obtaining a comprehensive view of enterprise risk.</p>\n<p>Risk reports may remain fragmented, making it difficult for senior management to determine whether the organisation's total exposure is acceptable.</p>\n<p>Third, risk-management activities may not be sufficiently connected to corporate strategy.</p>\n<p>Individual departments may focus on controlling their own exposures without evaluating how decisions affect broader organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>Fourth, the approach may create inconsistent risk assessments and duplicated management efforts.</p>\n<p>Different departments may use different reporting methods, assessment criteria or assumptions, reducing coordination.</p>\n<p>ERM addresses these limitations by adopting an integrated and holistic approach.</p>\n<p>It expands the scope of risk management beyond conventional financial and physical risks and encourages management to evaluate risks in relation to enterprise-wide objectives.</p>\n<p>ERM also facilitates a more comprehensive assessment of risk interactions and supports coordinated decision-making.</p>\n<p>However, implementing ERM may require stronger information systems, cross-functional cooperation, managerial commitment and additional resources.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the silo approach may remain useful for specialised risk analysis, but an integrated ERM perspective is important when material risks are interconnected or capable of affecting the organisation as a whole.</p>\n<p>The chapter contrasts conventional CRM's silo model with expanded, holistic risk management on pages 28, 30 and 38.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P8</span><span>Compare conventional Corporate Risk Management (CRM) with Enterprise Risk Management (ERM). <small class=\"muted\">(12 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Compare conventional Corporate Risk Management (CRM) with Enterprise Risk Management (ERM). Evaluate why ERM is regarded as a value-based management approach.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Conventional Corporate Risk Management and Enterprise Risk Management differ in their scope, orientation and relationship with organisational strategy.</p>\n<p>Conventional CRM, as presented in the lecture, focuses particularly on financial risks, including credit, market and liquidity exposures.</p>\n<p>These risks may be managed using financial instruments and techniques such as hedging, insurance, futures and options.</p>\n<p>Conventional CRM often follows a silo approach in which risks are managed separately by different functional departments.</p>\n<p>Although this approach can be effective for specific exposures, it may not fully address strategic, operational, reputational and other non-financial risks.</p>\n<p>ERM represents a broader orientation to corporate risk management.</p>\n<p>It seeks to identify and manage a wider range of material risks through an integrated and holistic framework.</p>\n<p>Rather than treating risks as independent problems, ERM considers their combined influence on enterprise objectives.</p>\n<p>ERM is regarded as a value-based management approach because its purpose extends beyond merely minimising losses.</p>\n<p>Effective ERM supports better decisions, appropriate risk-taking and the achievement of long-term organisational value.</p>\n<p>For example, a company considering international expansion should assess not only the foreign-exchange risks associated with the investment but also regulatory uncertainty, demand conditions, financing requirements, operational capacity and reputational exposure.</p>\n<p>A conventional financial-risk response may reduce currency volatility while leaving a commercially unsuccessful expansion strategy largely unchanged.</p>\n<p>ERM provides a broader framework for evaluating whether the expected opportunities justify the organisation's overall exposure.</p>\n<p>However, ERM does not eliminate uncertainty or guarantee successful decisions. Its effectiveness depends on the quality of risk information, organisational coordination and management judgement.</p>\n<p>Therefore, ERM is value-based because it integrates the management of risks and opportunities into the achievement of long-term enterprise objectives.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly identifies conventional CRM with credit, market and liquidity risk, hedging and silos, while presenting ERM as an expanded, holistic and value-based approach.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P9</span><span>A manufacturing company has adopted insurance and emergency-response procedures but has not assessed its strategic,... <small class=\"muted\">(8 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A manufacturing company has adopted insurance and emergency-response procedures but has not assessed its strategic, supply-chain or financial risks. Evaluate whether this constitutes effective Enterprise Risk Management.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>The company's existing arrangements demonstrate some aspects of traditional risk management but do not necessarily constitute a comprehensive ERM approach.</p>\n<p>Insurance may help the company finance certain financial losses arising from physical events such as fire or property damage.</p>\n<p>Emergency-response procedures may reduce the severity of certain incidents and support post-loss recovery.</p>\n<p>However, these measures primarily address selected loss exposures.</p>\n<p>They do not necessarily consider the organisation's broader strategic, operational and financial risks.</p>\n<p>For example, the company may face critical supplier dependencies, changes in customer demand, foreign-exchange volatility and funding liquidity problems.</p>\n<p>These exposures may threaten business performance even if no insurable physical loss occurs.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, such risks may be interconnected. A supply-chain disruption could reduce production, delay customer receipts and increase liquidity pressure.</p>\n<p>An effective ERM approach should therefore supplement existing insurance and emergency arrangements with a broader process for identifying, assessing, treating and monitoring material risks across the enterprise.</p>\n<p>Risk analysis should also be connected to strategic and operational objectives rather than confined to individual departmental exposures.</p>\n<p>Consequently, the company has implemented useful risk-control measures, but further integration would be required before management could reasonably claim to have a comprehensive ERM programme.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P10</span><span>Discuss why emerging sustainability and technological risks have encouraged organisations to adopt a more holistic... <small class=\"muted\">(8 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Discuss why emerging sustainability and technological risks have encouraged organisations to adopt a more holistic approach to corporate risk management.</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Modern organisations face risks that extend beyond traditional physical hazards and financial exposures.</p>\n<p>The lecture highlights the emergence of social and environmental risks, changing business conditions and additional compliance requirements as important developments affecting corporate risk management.</p>\n<p>Environmental issues may affect organisations through physical disruptions, resource availability, regulation and changing business conditions.</p>\n<p>Social issues may affect workforce conditions, stakeholder expectations and organisational reputation.</p>\n<p>These exposures can produce operational, strategic and financial consequences.</p>\n<p>For example, environmental damage may create regulatory costs, reputational problems and interruptions to production.</p>\n<p>Technological developments also influence the risk-management environment.</p>\n<p>On the one hand, data analytics and artificial intelligence can support better identification, modelling, monitoring and assessment of risk.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, greater technological dependence may create new exposures involving systems, information and operational processes.</p>\n<p>These developments reinforce the limitations of managing risks through narrow departmental silos.</p>\n<p>A more holistic approach allows organisations to consider a wider set of risk drivers and their potential interactions with strategy, governance, compliance and operational activities.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the increasing complexity of the business environment supports the use of integrated risk management that considers both emerging risks and their implications for long-term enterprise value.</p>\n<p>This answer follows the lecture's discussion of sustainability-related risks and the expanding scope of corporate risk management.</p>\n<p>The lecture's sustainability discussion relies partly on historical global-risk reports from 2015-2017. Those examples explain the chapter's evolution narrative; they should not be treated as a current ranking of global risks.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P11</span><span>ABC Manufacturing Sdn. <small class=\"muted\">(20 marks)</small></span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>ABC Manufacturing Sdn. Bhd. is a Malaysian company producing industrial components.</p>\n<p>Management plans to expand into Vietnam to increase market share and long-term shareholder wealth.</p>\n<p>The expansion requires substantial debt financing. The company depends on one overseas supplier for critical materials, and its production facilities use an outdated IT system.</p>\n<p>Meanwhile, the company's finance department, operations department and legal department assess risks independently and report directly to their respective functional managers.</p>\n<p>The board believes that purchasing adequate insurance and hedging foreign-exchange exposure will be sufficient to manage the risks associated with the expansion.</p>\n<p>Required:</p>\n<p>(a) Identify and explain four risks associated with the proposed expansion. (8 marks)</p>\n<p>(b) Analyse the limitations of the company's current risk-management approach. (4 marks)</p>\n<p>(c) Recommend an appropriate risk-management approach and justify your recommendation. (5 marks)</p>\n<p>(d) Explain how the company should consider pre-loss and post-loss objectives. (3 marks)</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h2>Model answer - (a) Risk identification and explanation (8 marks)</h2>\n<p>1. Strategic risk</p>\n<p>The company's decision to expand into Vietnam involves strategic risk because success depends on factors such as market demand, competition and the organisation's ability to achieve its international expansion objectives.</p>\n<p>If the new market does not generate sufficient revenue, the company may fail to increase shareholder wealth despite significant investment.</p>\n<p>2. Financial and liquidity risk</p>\n<p>Substantial debt financing may create pressure on the company's cash flows and its ability to meet repayment obligations.</p>\n<p>If the expansion produces lower-than-expected earnings, the company could experience funding liquidity problems.</p>\n<p>3. Operational and resource risk</p>\n<p>Dependence on a single supplier exposes the company to disruption if that supplier experiences delays or fails to deliver necessary materials.</p>\n<p>Additionally, outdated IT systems may contribute to operational failures that interrupt production and business activities.</p>\n<p>4. Compliance risk</p>\n<p>Expanding into Vietnam requires the company to satisfy applicable legal and regulatory requirements.</p>\n<p>Failure to comply with relevant business, labour or environmental regulations could create legal consequences, operational disruptions and additional expenses.</p>\n<p>The categories follow the lecture's strategic, tactical, operational and compliance framework. Financial and supplier exposures can also be discussed at tactical or operational levels depending on how the question is framed.</p>\n<h2>Model answer - (b) Limitations of the current approach (4 marks)</h2>\n<p>ABC Manufacturing currently follows a conventional silo-based approach because departments manage risks independently.</p>\n<p>Although individual departments may have relevant expertise, this structure may prevent the company from understanding relationships among its risks.</p>\n<p>For example, a supplier disruption may delay production, reducing revenue and worsening the company's ability to service its debt.</p>\n<p>At the same time, foreign-exchange movements may increase the cost of imported materials, further affecting profitability and cash flow.</p>\n<p>Insurance and foreign-exchange hedging may address specific exposures, but they do not necessarily manage strategic failure, IT disruption or the combined effect of multiple risks.</p>\n<p>Consequently, the company lacks a sufficiently comprehensive assessment of its enterprise-wide exposure.</p>\n<h2>Model answer - (c) Recommendation and justification (5 marks)</h2>\n<p>ABC Manufacturing should adopt an integrated Enterprise Risk Management approach before proceeding with the expansion.</p>\n<p>First, management should establish the expansion's objectives and identify the material strategic, financial, operational and compliance risks associated with the investment.</p>\n<p>Second, the company should assess potential interactions among risks instead of relying entirely on separate departmental assessments.</p>\n<p>Third, management should evaluate appropriate risk responses, including supplier diversification, IT-system improvements, financial contingency arrangements and compliance procedures.</p>\n<p>Fourth, management should ensure relevant risk information is communicated and monitored across business functions.</p>\n<p>Adopting ERM would support more informed strategic decisions by allowing management to evaluate the total risks associated with the expansion relative to the potential value created.</p>\n<p>However, ERM implementation also requires resources, coordination and reliable information. Therefore, the extent of integration should be proportionate to the company's risk profile and organisational complexity.</p>\n<h2>Model answer - (d) Pre-loss and post-loss objectives (3 marks)</h2>\n<p>Before implementing the expansion, ABC Manufacturing should address pre-loss objectives by preparing economically for potential losses, reducing uncertainty through appropriate planning and meeting relevant legal requirements.</p>\n<p>This may involve establishing preventive controls, financial contingencies and appropriate insurance arrangements.</p>\n<p>After a major adverse event, post-loss objectives should focus on maintaining organisational survival, restoring operations and stabilising earnings.</p>\n<p>For example, if a supplier failure interrupts production, the company should activate contingency arrangements to maintain customer deliveries and minimise financial disruption.</p>\n<p>Both sets of objectives are necessary because risk controls cannot guarantee that no adverse events will occur.</p></div></details></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l2\" id=\"t2s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">16 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Part 1 - How to answer Topic 2 subjective questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s1\" title=\"Part 1 - How to answer Topic 2 subjective questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P1</span>How to answer Topic 2 subjective questions</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 2 - Legal forms of business and their risk implications\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s2\" title=\"Part 2 - Legal forms of business and their risk implications\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P2</span>Legal forms and risk</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 3 - Corporate objectives and conflicting decisions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s3\" title=\"Part 3 - Corporate objectives and conflicting decisions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P3</span>Corporate objectives and conflicting decisions</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 4 - Strategic, tactical, operational and compliance risks\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s4\" title=\"Part 4 - Strategic, tactical, operational and compliance risks\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P4</span>Four levels of risk</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 5 - Purpose and process of risk management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s5\" title=\"Part 5 - Purpose and process of risk management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P5</span>Purpose and process of risk management</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 6 - Pre-loss and post-loss objectives\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s6\" title=\"Part 6 - Pre-loss and post-loss objectives\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P6</span>Pre-loss and post-loss objectives</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 7 - Evolution of corporate risk management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s7\" title=\"Part 7 - Evolution of corporate risk management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P7</span>Evolution of corporate risk management</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 8 - Limitations of silo risk management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s8\" title=\"Part 8 - Limitations of silo risk management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P8</span>Limitations of silo risk management</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 9 - Conventional CRM vs Enterprise Risk Management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s9\" title=\"Part 9 - Conventional CRM vs Enterprise Risk Management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P9</span>Conventional CRM vs Enterprise Risk Management</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 10 - Pre-loss, post-loss and ERM working together\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s10\" title=\"Part 10 - Pre-loss, post-loss and ERM working together\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P10</span>Pre-loss, post-loss and ERM working together</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 11 - Sustainability and technology in the evolution of RM\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s11\" title=\"Part 11 - Sustainability and technology in the evolution of RM\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P11</span>Sustainability and technology</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 12 - Comprehensive 20-mark ERM case study\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s12\" title=\"Part 12 - Comprehensive 20-mark ERM case study\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P12</span>Comprehensive 20-mark ERM case study</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 13 - Difficult conceptual traps in subjective exams\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s13\" title=\"Part 13 - Difficult conceptual traps in subjective exams\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P13</span>Difficult conceptual traps in subjective exams</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 14 - Topic 2 subjective practice paper\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s14\" title=\"Part 14 - Topic 2 subjective practice paper\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P14</span>Topic 2 subjective practice paper</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 15 - Highest-priority topics for revision\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s15\" title=\"Part 15 - Highest-priority topics for revision\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P15</span>Highest-priority topics for revision</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 16 - Last-minute revision sheet\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2s-s16\" title=\"Part 16 - Last-minute revision sheet\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P16</span>Last-minute revision sheet</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l2\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Topic 2 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>16 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h3>Subjective Exam Preparation | Enterprise Risk Management</h3>\n<p>Topic 2 is particularly suitable for subjective questions because the examiner can ask you not only to describe how risk management evolved, but also to explain why it evolved and evaluate whether a traditional or enterprise-wide approach is appropriate for a particular organisation.</p>\n<p>Your lecture covers four main areas: types of businesses and associated risks, organisational objectives, the principles and aims of risk management, and the evolution of corporate risk management.</p>\n<p>I would organise your preparation around one central argument:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Organisational Complexity → Interconnected Risks → Limitations of Traditional RM → Integrated ERM → Long-term Value\"><span>Organisational Complexity</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Interconnected Risks</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Limitations of Traditional RM</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Integrated ERM</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Long-term Value</span></div>\n<p>The following practice questions use suggested marks and marking schemes. They are original revision questions, not confirmed examination questions.</p><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 1 - How to answer Topic 2 subjective questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Topic 1 focused on identifying and classifying risk. Topic 2 requires you to explain how an organisation structures and manages its risks.</p>\n<p>A useful five-stage approach for longer answers is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Define the relevant concept. Establish what the legal structure, objective, risk level or management approach means.</li>\n<li>Explain its purpose. Why does the business need it? What problem does it address?</li>\n<li>Apply the concept. Connect it to a realistic company, decision or risk event.</li>\n<li>Analyse the consequences. Show interactions across the business rather than describing risks separately.</li>\n<li>Evaluate the trade-offs. Explain the strengths, limitations and suitability of a proposed response.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For example, imagine the question asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Evaluate whether a multinational corporation should adopt Enterprise Risk Management rather than conventional risk management.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A weak response would be:</p>\n<p>\"ERM is better because it manages all risks holistically.\"</p>\n<p>That gives the examiner a conclusion without enough reasoning.</p>\n<p>A stronger response would explain that conventional departmental management may overlook common risk drivers, such as geopolitical instability simultaneously affecting foreign-exchange exposure, suppliers, market demand and financing. ERM addresses this limitation by aggregating and considering material risks in relation to corporate objectives. However, its implementation also requires coordination, information-sharing and resources.</p>\n<p>The second response demonstrates analysis and evaluation, not just understanding.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 2 - Legal forms of business and their risk implications</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_structures\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Legal form moves the boundary of who absorbs the loss; it does not remove business risk.</figcaption></figure></div><h4>Question 1 - 10 marks</h4>\n<p>Compare the risk implications of sole proprietorships, partnerships, private limited companies and limited liability partnerships. Evaluate why a growing business might change its organisational structure.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>The legal structure of a business affects how ownership, liability, control and continuity risks are distributed among its owners.</p>\n<p>A sole proprietorship is owned and controlled by one individual. Its main advantages include simpler administration, direct decision-making and operational flexibility. However, the owner has unlimited liability and may be personally responsible for business debts. The business may also depend heavily on the owner's capital, managerial capability and continued involvement.</p>\n<p>A general partnership involves two or more owners who may contribute additional financial resources, expertise and management capabilities. Nevertheless, the lecture describes general partners as having unlimited liability, including joint and several responsibility for partnership debts. Partnerships may also experience disagreements, conflicts of interest and difficulties maintaining continuity.</p>\n<p>A private limited company (Sdn. Bhd.) is a separate legal entity from its shareholders. Shareholders generally have limited liability, meaning that their financial exposure is ordinarily limited to their investment. This creates a clearer separation between personal and corporate obligations.</p>\n<p>A limited liability partnership (LLP) combines partnership-style flexibility with limited-liability protection. This structure may be suitable for businesses that want shared management and a degree of legal separation between the firm and its partners.</p>\n<p>For a growing enterprise, changing its legal structure may help reduce personal liability exposure, improve organisational continuity and support additional investment.</p>\n<p>However, limited liability does not eliminate the company's underlying business risks. The company may still face financial losses, operational failures or insolvency.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the choice of business structure should be based on the owners' liability tolerance, financing requirements, management preferences and long-term organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>This comparison follows the liability and legal-entity distinctions presented on pages 4-5 and 16-18.</p>\n<h4>Suggested marking allocation</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sole proprietorship and unlimited liability</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Partnership and shared responsibilities</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sdn. Bhd. and separate legal entity</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LLP and limited liability</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation of growth-related trade-offs</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Why this is an evaluation-level answer</h4>\n<p>The examiner is not just interested in whether you know that a Sdn. Bhd. has limited liability.</p>\n<p>The important insight is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Changing legal structure changes risk allocation, not necessarily the underlying business exposure.</span></div>\n<p>A company can protect shareholders from ordinary company obligations while still operating an extremely risky business.</p>\n<p>Scope note: Your slides use some US legal-entity and taxation comparisons alongside Malaysian structures. Treat those as lecture illustrations rather than assuming US LLC tax rules or limited-partnership arrangements apply identically in Malaysia. Likewise, limited liability is generally subject to relevant legal exceptions and personal guarantees.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 3 - Corporate objectives and conflicting decisions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Question 2 - 8 marks</h4>\n<p>Discuss the major objectives of a corporation. Explain why maximising short-term profit may conflict with long-term shareholder wealth and sustainability.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Corporations pursue several objectives, including survival, competitiveness, sales growth, profit maximisation, cost minimisation, market-share growth and shareholder-wealth maximisation.</p>\n<p>These objectives are interconnected but may conflict.</p>\n<p>For example, a company may minimise costs by reducing employee training, maintenance expenditure and quality-control activities.</p>\n<p>Although this decision may increase short-term accounting profit, it could also increase the frequency of operational failures, product defects and workplace accidents.</p>\n<p>These events may subsequently create financial losses, regulatory problems and reputational damage, potentially reducing long-term shareholder value.</p>\n<p>Similarly, a company may increase market share by aggressively reducing prices. Although sales volume may increase, profit margins may decline.</p>\n<p>The corporation must therefore evaluate decisions against multiple objectives rather than assuming that maximising one performance indicator automatically improves every other objective.</p>\n<p>The chapter also introduces the Triple Bottom Line, which considers People, Planet and Profit. This broadens the assessment of corporate performance beyond current financial results to include social and environmental considerations.</p>\n<p>Consequently, sound risk management should help companies balance short-term financial objectives with business continuity, competitiveness, stakeholder interests and long-term value creation.</p>\n<p>The lecture identifies these corporate objectives on page 20 and contrasts the single financial bottom line with the Triple Bottom Line on page 21.</p>\n<h4>Higher-order reasoning</h4>\n<p>Consider two hypothetical decisions:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Decision</th>\n<th>Immediate benefit</th>\n<th>Potential longer-term risk</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Reduce maintenance spending</td>\n<td>Lower expenses</td>\n<td>Equipment failure and production interruption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Invest in preventive maintenance</td>\n<td>Higher immediate expenses</td>\n<td>Lower probability of breakdowns</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Would preventive maintenance always be preferable?</p>\n<p>Not necessarily. Its benefits still need to justify the investment.</p>\n<p>But neither should management automatically choose the option with the lowest immediate cost.</p>\n<p>The appropriate question is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Which decision creates the greatest sustainable value, considering its risks and costs?</span></div>\n<p>This is why long-term shareholder-wealth maximisation is conceptually different from maximising this year's profit.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 4 - Strategic, tactical, operational and compliance risks</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_risk_levels\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Risk viewed at four levels, from direction-setting down to the rules the firm must follow.</figcaption></figure></div><h4>Question 3 - 8 marks</h4>\n<p>Differentiate between strategic, tactical, operational and compliance risks. Explain how these risk levels may interact when a company enters a foreign market.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Enterprise risks can be examined at different organisational levels to support risk identification, management and aggregation.</p>\n<p>Strategic risk concerns uncertainty affecting the organisation's long-term direction and ability to achieve its strategic objectives.</p>\n<p>For example, a Malaysian company may decide to enter the Indonesian market. The expansion could fail because management misjudges customer demand or competitive conditions.</p>\n<p>Tactical risk concerns uncertainty affecting plans and decisions used to implement strategic objectives.</p>\n<p>For example, management must determine how to finance the expansion, establish distribution arrangements and implement marketing strategies.</p>\n<p>Operational risk concerns uncertainty affecting day-to-day business activities and the execution of operational plans.</p>\n<p>For example, supply interruptions, system failures or inadequate employee training may prevent the new business from operating efficiently.</p>\n<p>Compliance risk concerns exposure arising from legal and regulatory requirements.</p>\n<p>For example, the company must comply with relevant foreign licensing requirements, labour regulations and business laws.</p>\n<p>Although these risks are classified at different levels, they are interconnected.</p>\n<p>A compliance failure may interrupt operations, increase costs and prevent management from achieving its strategic expansion objective.</p>\n<p>Therefore, examining risks at different levels allows management to identify potential exposures while understanding how failures in one area can affect the entire enterprise.</p>\n<p>This follows the four-level risk diagram on page 23, which distinguishes strategic, tactical, operational and compliance risk.</p>\n<h4>The causal logic</h4>\n<p>Strategic objective</p>\n<p>Enter Indonesia and expand market share.</p>\n<p>Tactical plan</p>\n<p>Arrange financing, suppliers, distribution and marketing.</p>\n<p>Operational execution</p>\n<p>Produce, transport and deliver goods reliably.</p>\n<p>Compliance requirements</p>\n<p>Ensure activities meet applicable local laws and regulations.</p>\n<p>The diagram illustrates organisational levels, not a strict chronological sequence. Compliance and operational requirements apply throughout the strategy's implementation.</p>\n<h4>Common marking mistake</h4>\n<p>Do not assume a type of risk belongs exclusively to one level.</p>\n<p>For instance, cybersecurity could involve day-to-day IT operations but also threaten a company's strategic digital-transformation objectives.</p>\n<p>The lecture's categories help organise analysis; they are not perfectly isolated compartments.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 5 - Purpose and process of risk management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Question 4 - 7 marks</h4>\n<p>Define risk management and explain how a systematic risk-management process can improve organisational decision-making and resource allocation.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Risk management is a logical and systematic process of identifying, analysing, treating and monitoring risks associated with organisational activities.</p>\n<p>Its purpose is not merely to reduce financial losses but also to improve decision-making, identify opportunities and ensure that available resources are used effectively.</p>\n<p>The process begins with risk identification, where management determines potential events or conditions that may affect business objectives.</p>\n<p>Next, risks are analysed and evaluated to determine their significance and prioritise those requiring management attention.</p>\n<p>Management then selects appropriate risk treatments to reduce or otherwise address relevant exposures.</p>\n<p>Finally, risks and controls are monitored and reviewed to determine whether the responses remain effective as circumstances change.</p>\n<p>A systematic process improves resource allocation because management can direct attention and funds towards risks that could have the greatest impact on organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>For example, a manufacturer facing both frequent minor equipment failures and a potentially catastrophic production interruption should evaluate the relative consequences of these exposures before deciding where to invest in controls.</p>\n<p>Therefore, risk management improves organisational decisions by replacing ad hoc responses with structured analysis, prioritisation, treatment and monitoring.</p>\n<p>The lecture defines risk management as a systematic method and explicitly links it with better decisions, opportunity identification and more effective resource utilisation.</p>\n<h4>What makes this more than a definition?</h4>\n<p>A weak response would list four process stages and stop.</p>\n<p>The stronger answer explains why the process matters:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Assessment → Prioritisation → Better Resource Allocation\"><span>Assessment</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Prioritisation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Better Resource Allocation</span></div>\n<p>That causal explanation earns credit in questions using verbs such as discuss, analyse and evaluate.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 6 - Pre-loss and post-loss objectives</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Question 5 - 8 marks</h4>\n<p>Differentiate between the pre-loss and post-loss objectives of risk management. Explain why organisations need both.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Risk management has objectives both before and after a loss occurs.</p>\n<p>Pre-loss objectives are concerned with preparing for potential losses in the most economical manner, reducing anxiety associated with uncertain events and complying with relevant legal obligations.</p>\n<p>For example, a manufacturing company may conduct safety training, install fire-protection systems and purchase appropriate insurance before experiencing a major loss.</p>\n<p>These measures help prepare the organisation for potential risk events.</p>\n<p>Post-loss objectives focus on the organisation's ability to survive and recover after a loss occurs.</p>\n<p>They include ensuring business survival, maintaining or restoring operations, stabilising earnings, supporting continued growth and minimising adverse consequences for individuals or society.</p>\n<p>For example, after a fire damages its production facility, a company may activate alternative production arrangements and use available financial resources to maintain essential business functions.</p>\n<p>Both categories are important because preventive preparation cannot guarantee that adverse events will never occur.</p>\n<p>A company may successfully reduce the probability of a fire while still remaining exposed to serious damage if a fire occurs.</p>\n<p>Therefore, effective risk management should balance economical preparation before losses with organisational continuity and recovery after losses.</p>\n<p>These objectives are expressly divided into pre-loss and post-loss categories on page 26.</p>\n<h4>Suggested marking scheme</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain pre-loss objectives</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain post-loss objectives</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relevant business examples</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explain why both are necessary</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Evaluation-level distinction</h4>\n<p>A business-continuity plan may be prepared before a disaster but is designed to achieve continuity after the disaster.</p>\n<p>Therefore, distinguish the timing of a risk-management activity from the ultimate objective it serves.</p>\n<p>That is a subtle point an examiner can test.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 7 - Evolution of corporate risk management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_rm_evolution\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Risks accumulated rather than disappeared, so the management approach had to widen.</figcaption></figure></div><h4>Question 6 - 12 marks</h4>\n<p>Trace the evolution of corporate risk management from traditional insurance-based approaches to Enterprise Risk Management. Explain why this evolution became necessary.</p>\n<p>This is one of the highest-priority long-answer questions in Topic 2.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Corporate risk management has evolved from a narrow insurance-based discipline into a more integrated approach that considers a broad range of enterprise risks in relation to long-term organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>First, traditional risk management focused primarily on pure risks and insurance.</p>\n<p>Historically, organisations concentrated on exposures such as fire, theft and natural disasters.</p>\n<p>Insurance was used to transfer or finance some of the financial consequences arising from these events.</p>\n<p>However, this approach placed relatively little emphasis on many uninsurable risks, particularly those arising from business strategy, organisational processes and changing market conditions.</p>\n<p>Second, organisations expanded their focus towards financial risk management.</p>\n<p>As businesses became larger and more complex, management increasingly addressed risks involving credit, liquidity, interest rates and foreign-exchange fluctuations.</p>\n<p>Techniques such as hedging, futures and options became important tools for managing certain financial exposures.</p>\n<p>Nevertheless, risks were often managed through departmental or functional silos.</p>\n<p>For example, a finance department might manage exchange-rate exposure independently while an operations department managed supply-chain risk.</p>\n<p>Third, integrated risk management emerged as organisations recognised that risks could not always be managed independently.</p>\n<p>Organisations began incorporating risk considerations into strategic planning and business decision-making rather than treating risk management exclusively as a reactive function.</p>\n<p>This broader approach included operational, reputational and strategic risks.</p>\n<p>Fourth, Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) developed as a holistic, enterprise-wide approach.</p>\n<p>ERM seeks to identify and manage a wider spectrum of material risks in an integrated manner.</p>\n<p>Instead of evaluating every exposure independently, management considers relationships among risks and their collective implications for corporate objectives.</p>\n<p>For example, geopolitical instability may simultaneously affect suppliers, foreign-exchange costs, customer demand and liquidity.</p>\n<p>Under a silo approach, these risks may appear manageable individually while their combined impact threatens business continuity.</p>\n<p>Finally, the chapter describes a further movement toward holistic risk management.</p>\n<p>This approach increasingly incorporates broader organisational concerns, including sustainability, governance, compliance and emerging technological risks.</p>\n<p>Risk management becomes more closely integrated with the firm's wider management and decision-making activities.</p>\n<p>Overall, this evolution became necessary because modern organisations face increasingly complex and interconnected risks that cannot be adequately addressed through insurance or isolated departmental controls alone.</p>\n<p>Consequently, ERM provides a broader approach to managing uncertainty while supporting strategic objectives and long-term enterprise value.</p>\n<p>The lecture presents this evolution across pages 27-32, with ERM explicitly characterised as a value-based, holistic management approach.</p>\n<h4>Suggested marking scheme</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Traditional insurance-based approach</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Development of financial risk management</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Silo limitations</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integrated risk management</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ERM and value creation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Holistic development and justification</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>The most important reasoning to memorise</h4>\n<p>Traditional RM</p>\n<p>Problem addressed: How can the financial consequences of individual losses be managed?</p>\n<p>Financial / Conventional CRM</p>\n<p>Problem addressed: How can credit, market and liquidity exposures be controlled?</p>\n<p>Integrated RM</p>\n<p>Problem addressed: How can risk analysis become part of planning and decision-making?</p>\n<p>ERM</p>\n<p>Problem addressed: How can all material risks be managed collectively in support of enterprise objectives?</p>\n<p>Holistic RM</p>\n<p>Broader integration with strategy, governance, sustainability, compliance and emerging risks.</p>\n<p>The stages are a useful interpretation of the lecture's diagrams, not universally agreed, mutually exclusive historical periods. Organisations may still use traditional, financial and holistic approaches simultaneously.</p>\n<p>The key to a strong answer is explaining why the next stage emerged, rather than only naming each stage.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 8 - Limitations of silo risk management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t2_silo_vs_erm\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Silo view versus enterprise view</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Four departments each report a 10% chance of a loss. Turn up the shared dependency and watch the chance of several hitting at once.</figcaption></figure></div><h4>Question 7 - 10 marks</h4>\n<p>Critically evaluate the limitations of the traditional silo approach to corporate risk management. Explain how ERM addresses these limitations.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>The silo approach refers to managing risks separately within different functional departments or business units.</p>\n<p>For example, the finance department may manage credit and liquidity risks, the IT department may manage technological risks, and the operations department may manage production-related risks.</p>\n<p>This approach provides certain advantages because specialist departments possess technical expertise and detailed knowledge of their respective exposures.</p>\n<p>However, silo risk management has several limitations.</p>\n<p>First, it may fail to identify risk interdependencies.</p>\n<p>Risks originating in different departments may share common underlying drivers.</p>\n<p>For example, geopolitical instability could simultaneously increase foreign-exchange costs, interrupt suppliers and reduce customer demand.</p>\n<p>Although each department may assess its risk independently, the collective exposure could be substantially greater than expected.</p>\n<p>Second, silo management may prevent management from obtaining a comprehensive view of enterprise risk.</p>\n<p>Risk reports may remain fragmented, making it difficult for senior management to determine whether the organisation's total exposure is acceptable.</p>\n<p>Third, risk-management activities may not be sufficiently connected to corporate strategy.</p>\n<p>Individual departments may focus on controlling their own exposures without evaluating how decisions affect broader organisational objectives.</p>\n<p>Fourth, the approach may create inconsistent risk assessments and duplicated management efforts.</p>\n<p>Different departments may use different reporting methods, assessment criteria or assumptions, reducing coordination.</p>\n<p>ERM addresses these limitations by adopting an integrated and holistic approach.</p>\n<p>It expands the scope of risk management beyond conventional financial and physical risks and encourages management to evaluate risks in relation to enterprise-wide objectives.</p>\n<p>ERM also facilitates a more comprehensive assessment of risk interactions and supports coordinated decision-making.</p>\n<p>However, implementing ERM may require stronger information systems, cross-functional cooperation, managerial commitment and additional resources.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the silo approach may remain useful for specialised risk analysis, but an integrated ERM perspective is important when material risks are interconnected or capable of affecting the organisation as a whole.</p>\n<p>The chapter contrasts conventional CRM's silo model with expanded, holistic risk management on pages 28, 30 and 38.</p>\n<h4>Why the final qualification matters</h4>\n<p>Avoid making the simplistic argument that specialist departments are inherently bad.</p>\n<p>A finance department should still have people who understand foreign exchange.</p>\n<p>An IT department should still have people who understand cybersecurity.</p>\n<p>The problem arises when:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Specialisation becomes isolation.</span></div>\n<p>The strongest ERM structure combines specialised risk expertise with enterprise-level coordination.</p>\n<h4>Advanced analytical insight</h4>\n<p>Imagine three hypothetical risks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_1=\\text{Foreign-exchange exposure}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_2=\\text{Supplier disruption}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_3=\\text{Demand decline}$$</div>\n<p>Individually, each department concludes its risk is manageable.</p>\n<p>But all three depend on a common geopolitical event.</p>\n<p>That event could cause all three problems simultaneously.</p>\n<p>Therefore, enterprise risk cannot always be understood by looking at isolated departmental risk ratings.</p>\n<p>This common-risk-driver example is an analytical extension of the lecture's silo-versus-ERM discussion, rather than a mathematical aggregation model prescribed in the slides.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 9 - Conventional CRM vs Enterprise Risk Management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Question 8 - 12 marks</h4>\n<p>Compare conventional Corporate Risk Management (CRM) with Enterprise Risk Management (ERM). Evaluate why ERM is regarded as a value-based management approach.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Conventional Corporate Risk Management and Enterprise Risk Management differ in their scope, orientation and relationship with organisational strategy.</p>\n<p>Conventional CRM, as presented in the lecture, focuses particularly on financial risks, including credit, market and liquidity exposures.</p>\n<p>These risks may be managed using financial instruments and techniques such as hedging, insurance, futures and options.</p>\n<p>Conventional CRM often follows a silo approach in which risks are managed separately by different functional departments.</p>\n<p>Although this approach can be effective for specific exposures, it may not fully address strategic, operational, reputational and other non-financial risks.</p>\n<p>ERM represents a broader orientation to corporate risk management.</p>\n<p>It seeks to identify and manage a wider range of material risks through an integrated and holistic framework.</p>\n<p>Rather than treating risks as independent problems, ERM considers their combined influence on enterprise objectives.</p>\n<p>ERM is regarded as a value-based management approach because its purpose extends beyond merely minimising losses.</p>\n<p>Effective ERM supports better decisions, appropriate risk-taking and the achievement of long-term organisational value.</p>\n<p>For example, a company considering international expansion should assess not only the foreign-exchange risks associated with the investment but also regulatory uncertainty, demand conditions, financing requirements, operational capacity and reputational exposure.</p>\n<p>A conventional financial-risk response may reduce currency volatility while leaving a commercially unsuccessful expansion strategy largely unchanged.</p>\n<p>ERM provides a broader framework for evaluating whether the expected opportunities justify the organisation's overall exposure.</p>\n<p>However, ERM does not eliminate uncertainty or guarantee successful decisions. Its effectiveness depends on the quality of risk information, organisational coordination and management judgement.</p>\n<p>Therefore, ERM is value-based because it integrates the management of risks and opportunities into the achievement of long-term enterprise objectives.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly identifies conventional CRM with credit, market and liquidity risk, hedging and silos, while presenting ERM as an expanded, holistic and value-based approach.</p>\n<h4>Comparison table</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Conventional CRM</th>\n<th>ERM</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary focus</td>\n<td>Predominantly financial exposures in the lecture</td>\n<td>Broad spectrum of material enterprise risks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Approach</td>\n<td>Often departmental/silo</td>\n<td>Holistic and integrated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical tools</td>\n<td>Hedging, insurance, futures, options</td>\n<td>Coordinated risk identification, assessment and treatment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strategic connection</td>\n<td>May be relatively limited</td>\n<td>Explicit connection to objectives and decisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main emphasis</td>\n<td>Managing particular exposures</td>\n<td>Supporting enterprise-wide decisions and long-term value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Potential weakness</td>\n<td>May overlook risk interactions</td>\n<td>Greater coordination and implementation demands</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>What does \"value-based\" really mean?</h4>\n<p>Suppose a company faces two possible investments.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Investment</th>\n<th>Expected benefit</th>\n<th>Risk</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>Modest</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>Potentially substantial</td>\n<td>Higher</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A narrow interpretation of risk management might automatically prefer A.</p>\n<p>ERM should not do that without analysis.</p>\n<p>Instead, management asks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Are the expected benefits of B sufficient to justify its risks?</span></div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Can those risks be managed within the organisation&#x27;s capacity?</span></div>\n<p>This is why ERM is not synonymous with eliminating risk.</p>\n<p>An enterprise that refuses every uncertain project may avoid certain losses but sacrifice valuable opportunities for growth.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 10 - Pre-loss, post-loss and ERM working together</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Question 9 - 8 marks</h4>\n<p>A manufacturing company has adopted insurance and emergency-response procedures but has not assessed its strategic, supply-chain or financial risks. Evaluate whether this constitutes effective Enterprise Risk Management.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>The company's existing arrangements demonstrate some aspects of traditional risk management but do not necessarily constitute a comprehensive ERM approach.</p>\n<p>Insurance may help the company finance certain financial losses arising from physical events such as fire or property damage.</p>\n<p>Emergency-response procedures may reduce the severity of certain incidents and support post-loss recovery.</p>\n<p>However, these measures primarily address selected loss exposures.</p>\n<p>They do not necessarily consider the organisation's broader strategic, operational and financial risks.</p>\n<p>For example, the company may face critical supplier dependencies, changes in customer demand, foreign-exchange volatility and funding liquidity problems.</p>\n<p>These exposures may threaten business performance even if no insurable physical loss occurs.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, such risks may be interconnected. A supply-chain disruption could reduce production, delay customer receipts and increase liquidity pressure.</p>\n<p>An effective ERM approach should therefore supplement existing insurance and emergency arrangements with a broader process for identifying, assessing, treating and monitoring material risks across the enterprise.</p>\n<p>Risk analysis should also be connected to strategic and operational objectives rather than confined to individual departmental exposures.</p>\n<p>Consequently, the company has implemented useful risk-control measures, but further integration would be required before management could reasonably claim to have a comprehensive ERM programme.</p>\n<h4>Examiner's trap</h4>\n<p>Do not argue that insurance is useless.</p>\n<p>The better conclusion is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Insurance can be one component of risk management, but insurance alone is not ERM.</span></div>\n<p>A high-scoring answer recognises the value of existing controls while identifying what is missing.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 11 - Sustainability and technology in the evolution of RM</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Question 10 - 8 marks</h4>\n<p>Discuss why emerging sustainability and technological risks have encouraged organisations to adopt a more holistic approach to corporate risk management.</p>\n<h4>Model answer</h4>\n<p>Modern organisations face risks that extend beyond traditional physical hazards and financial exposures.</p>\n<p>The lecture highlights the emergence of social and environmental risks, changing business conditions and additional compliance requirements as important developments affecting corporate risk management.</p>\n<p>Environmental issues may affect organisations through physical disruptions, resource availability, regulation and changing business conditions.</p>\n<p>Social issues may affect workforce conditions, stakeholder expectations and organisational reputation.</p>\n<p>These exposures can produce operational, strategic and financial consequences.</p>\n<p>For example, environmental damage may create regulatory costs, reputational problems and interruptions to production.</p>\n<p>Technological developments also influence the risk-management environment.</p>\n<p>On the one hand, data analytics and artificial intelligence can support better identification, modelling, monitoring and assessment of risk.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, greater technological dependence may create new exposures involving systems, information and operational processes.</p>\n<p>These developments reinforce the limitations of managing risks through narrow departmental silos.</p>\n<p>A more holistic approach allows organisations to consider a wider set of risk drivers and their potential interactions with strategy, governance, compliance and operational activities.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the increasing complexity of the business environment supports the use of integrated risk management that considers both emerging risks and their implications for long-term enterprise value.</p>\n<p>This answer follows the lecture's discussion of sustainability-related risks and the expanding scope of corporate risk management.</p>\n<p>The lecture's sustainability discussion relies partly on historical global-risk reports from 2015-2017. Those examples explain the chapter's evolution narrative; they should not be treated as a current ranking of global risks.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 12 - Comprehensive 20-mark ERM case study</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This case combines the main concepts in Topic 2. It is the kind of question that can test almost the entire chapter through a single business scenario.</p>\n<h3>Question 11 - 20 marks</h3>\n<p>ABC Manufacturing Sdn. Bhd. is a Malaysian company producing industrial components.</p>\n<p>Management plans to expand into Vietnam to increase market share and long-term shareholder wealth.</p>\n<p>The expansion requires substantial debt financing. The company depends on one overseas supplier for critical materials, and its production facilities use an outdated IT system.</p>\n<p>Meanwhile, the company's finance department, operations department and legal department assess risks independently and report directly to their respective functional managers.</p>\n<p>The board believes that purchasing adequate insurance and hedging foreign-exchange exposure will be sufficient to manage the risks associated with the expansion.</p>\n<p>Required:</p>\n<p>(a) Identify and explain four risks associated with the proposed expansion. (8 marks)</p>\n<p>(b) Analyse the limitations of the company's current risk-management approach. (4 marks)</p>\n<p>(c) Recommend an appropriate risk-management approach and justify your recommendation. (5 marks)</p>\n<p>(d) Explain how the company should consider pre-loss and post-loss objectives. (3 marks)</p>\n<h3>Model answer - (a) Risk identification and explanation (8 marks)</h3>\n<p>1. Strategic risk</p>\n<p>The company's decision to expand into Vietnam involves strategic risk because success depends on factors such as market demand, competition and the organisation's ability to achieve its international expansion objectives.</p>\n<p>If the new market does not generate sufficient revenue, the company may fail to increase shareholder wealth despite significant investment.</p>\n<p>2. Financial and liquidity risk</p>\n<p>Substantial debt financing may create pressure on the company's cash flows and its ability to meet repayment obligations.</p>\n<p>If the expansion produces lower-than-expected earnings, the company could experience funding liquidity problems.</p>\n<p>3. Operational and resource risk</p>\n<p>Dependence on a single supplier exposes the company to disruption if that supplier experiences delays or fails to deliver necessary materials.</p>\n<p>Additionally, outdated IT systems may contribute to operational failures that interrupt production and business activities.</p>\n<p>4. Compliance risk</p>\n<p>Expanding into Vietnam requires the company to satisfy applicable legal and regulatory requirements.</p>\n<p>Failure to comply with relevant business, labour or environmental regulations could create legal consequences, operational disruptions and additional expenses.</p>\n<p>The categories follow the lecture's strategic, tactical, operational and compliance framework. Financial and supplier exposures can also be discussed at tactical or operational levels depending on how the question is framed.</p>\n<h3>Model answer - (b) Limitations of the current approach (4 marks)</h3>\n<p>ABC Manufacturing currently follows a conventional silo-based approach because departments manage risks independently.</p>\n<p>Although individual departments may have relevant expertise, this structure may prevent the company from understanding relationships among its risks.</p>\n<p>For example, a supplier disruption may delay production, reducing revenue and worsening the company's ability to service its debt.</p>\n<p>At the same time, foreign-exchange movements may increase the cost of imported materials, further affecting profitability and cash flow.</p>\n<p>Insurance and foreign-exchange hedging may address specific exposures, but they do not necessarily manage strategic failure, IT disruption or the combined effect of multiple risks.</p>\n<p>Consequently, the company lacks a sufficiently comprehensive assessment of its enterprise-wide exposure.</p>\n<h3>Model answer - (c) Recommendation and justification (5 marks)</h3>\n<p>ABC Manufacturing should adopt an integrated Enterprise Risk Management approach before proceeding with the expansion.</p>\n<p>First, management should establish the expansion's objectives and identify the material strategic, financial, operational and compliance risks associated with the investment.</p>\n<p>Second, the company should assess potential interactions among risks instead of relying entirely on separate departmental assessments.</p>\n<p>Third, management should evaluate appropriate risk responses, including supplier diversification, IT-system improvements, financial contingency arrangements and compliance procedures.</p>\n<p>Fourth, management should ensure relevant risk information is communicated and monitored across business functions.</p>\n<p>Adopting ERM would support more informed strategic decisions by allowing management to evaluate the total risks associated with the expansion relative to the potential value created.</p>\n<p>However, ERM implementation also requires resources, coordination and reliable information. Therefore, the extent of integration should be proportionate to the company's risk profile and organisational complexity.</p>\n<h3>Model answer - (d) Pre-loss and post-loss objectives (3 marks)</h3>\n<p>Before implementing the expansion, ABC Manufacturing should address pre-loss objectives by preparing economically for potential losses, reducing uncertainty through appropriate planning and meeting relevant legal requirements.</p>\n<p>This may involve establishing preventive controls, financial contingencies and appropriate insurance arrangements.</p>\n<p>After a major adverse event, post-loss objectives should focus on maintaining organisational survival, restoring operations and stabilising earnings.</p>\n<p>For example, if a supplier failure interrupts production, the company should activate contingency arrangements to maintain customer deliveries and minimise financial disruption.</p>\n<p>Both sets of objectives are necessary because risk controls cannot guarantee that no adverse events will occur.</p>\n<h3>Why this is a strong 20-mark answer</h3>\n<p>The answer demonstrates four levels of reasoning:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>Demonstrated through</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Understand</td>\n<td>Correctly explaining ERM terminology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apply</td>\n<td>Classifying risks in the Vietnamese expansion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analyse</td>\n<td>Tracing supplier, production, revenue and liquidity consequences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluate/Create</td>\n<td>Recommending an integrated ERM approach and justifying its design</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Most importantly, the recommendation is tied to the company's specific circumstances.</p>\n<p>A generic statement such as \"The company should implement ERM because ERM is holistic\" would be much weaker.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 13 - Difficult conceptual traps in subjective exams</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h3>Trap 1: ERM does not eliminate departmental risk management</h3>\n<p>A weak answer might say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Under ERM, individual departments should no longer manage their own risks.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is not the correct implication of the lecture.</p>\n<p>ERM requires better integration rather than eliminating specialised expertise.</p>\n<p>The finance department still manages financial exposures. Operations still manages production exposures.</p>\n<p>ERM allows those assessments to be considered together when they affect the enterprise.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ERM integrates specialisation; it does not necessarily replace it.</span></div>\n<h3>Trap 2: ERM does not automatically reduce every risk</h3>\n<p>Suppose an organisation accepts a calculated risk to enter a new market.</p>\n<p>Could this still be consistent with ERM?</p>\n<p>Yes.</p>\n<p>ERM is described as value-based. Management may accept some exposures when the expected opportunities justify the risks and the decision supports corporate objectives.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>More risk is not automatically poor risk management.</span></div>\n<p>The question is whether the exposure is understood and appropriately managed.</p>\n<h3>Trap 3: Diversification is not the same as integration</h3>\n<p>These ideas are related but distinct.</p>\n<p>Diversification spreads exposure across different investments, activities or sources.</p>\n<p>Integration considers risks and their interdependencies in a coordinated decision-making process.</p>\n<p>For example, using several independent suppliers is a diversification measure.</p>\n<p>Assessing how supplier risks interact with production, financing and strategic objectives is an ERM integration activity.</p>\n<h3>Trap 4: Legal structure does not determine risk-management quality</h3>\n<p>A company with limited liability can still have poor risk governance, weak financial controls or disastrous strategic decisions.</p>\n<p>Limited liability describes how certain obligations are allocated, not how effectively those risks are managed.</p>\n<h3>Trap 5: Short-term profitability and long-term value are not identical</h3>\n<p>A company may reduce operating expenditure and increase this year's profit while exposing itself to higher future production losses.</p>\n<p>The correct answer should acknowledge the trade-off rather than assuming that every cost reduction is beneficial.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 14 - Topic 2 subjective practice paper</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h3>Practice examination</h3>\n<p>60 marks</p>\n<p>Suggested duration: 75 minutes. Select a question, write your answer, and submit it for a marking-based critique.</p>\n<p>Select a questionQ1 · Business structure</p>\n<p>8 marks</p>\n<p>10 min suggested</p>\n<h4>A two-person business partnership is expanding and its owners are concerned about personal liability, financing and business continuity. Compare a general partnership, Sdn. Bhd. and LLP from a risk-management perspective. Recommend a suitable direction, explaining your assumptions and limitations.</h4>\n<p>Your answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking points Grade my answer</p>\n<p>Expected marking elements</p>\n<p>Characteristics of partnership and shared/unlimited liability</p>\n<p>Sdn. Bhd. separate entity and generally limited shareholder liability</p>\n<p>LLP flexibility and limited liability</p>\n<p>Continuity and management implications</p>\n<p>Balanced recommendation based on objectives and exposures</p>\n<p>These are suggested criteria. Exact mark allocations depend on the examiner's rubric.</p>\n<h4>A listed manufacturer increases short-term profit by reducing worker training, product-quality controls and machine maintenance. Using the goals of the corporation and the Triple Bottom Line, evaluate whether this decision is consistent with long-term shareholder-wealth maximisation.</h4>\n<p>Your answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking points Grade my answer\nExpected marking elements\nIdentify corporate financial and nonfinancial objectives</p>\n<p>Differentiate short-term profit and long-term value</p>\n<p>Analyse consequences for operations, social outcomes and reputation</p>\n<p>Apply People, Planet, Profit where relevant</p>\n<p>Provide justified evaluation instead of an automatic yes/no</p>\n<p>These are suggested criteria. Exact mark allocations depend on the examiner's rubric.</p>\n<h4>A retail group launches an e-commerce strategy, chooses a third-party payment provider, experiences repeated system failures and subsequently violates consumer-data requirements. Analyse the strategic, tactical, operational and compliance dimensions and explain the relationships among them.</h4>\n<p>Your answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking points Grade my answer\nExpected marking elements\nCorrect strategic direction identified</p>\n<p>Tactical execution decision explained</p>\n<p>Operational system failure identified</p>\n<p>Compliance dimension explained</p>\n<p>Risk interdependencies and effects on objectives</p>\n<p>These are suggested criteria. Exact mark allocations depend on the examiner's rubric.</p>\n<h4>A manufacturing plant experiences an unexpected major fire. Critically discuss the pre-loss and post-loss objectives of risk management, and recommend how the organisation should balance preventive preparation with operational recovery.</h4>\n<p>Your answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking points Grade my answer\nExpected marking elements\nPre-loss economical preparation, anxiety, legal obligations</p>\n<p>Post-loss survival, continuity, earnings, growth and social impacts</p>\n<p>Appropriate manufacturing-specific measures</p>\n<p>Distinguish preparation timing from recovery objectives</p>\n<p>Reasoned balance and limits of controls</p>\n<p>These are suggested criteria. Exact mark allocations depend on the examiner's rubric.</p>\n<h4>Traditional corporate risk management was often insurance-based and managed in silos. Explain the evolution toward integrated risk management and ERM. Critically evaluate why the silo approach may be insufficient for a multinational company.</h4>\n<p>Your answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking points Grade my answer\nExpected marking elements\nInsurance and pure-risk origins</p>\n<p>Expansion toward conventional financial risk management</p>\n<p>Silo characteristics and specialist strengths</p>\n<p>Interdependencies and aggregate enterprise exposure</p>\n<p>Integrated/ERM evolution, strategic links and long-term value</p>\n<p>Practical limitations of ERM implementation</p>\n<p>These are suggested criteria. Exact mark allocations depend on the examiner's rubric.</p>\n<h4>A Malaysian energy company is expanding into a foreign market. It uses debt financing, depends on a limited number of suppliers and faces changing environmental regulations and technological uncertainty. Each department assesses risks separately. Recommend a risk-management approach and explain how your recommendation supports long-term enterprise value.</h4>\n<p>Your answer</p>\n<p>Hide marking points Grade my answer\nExpected marking elements\nIdentify strategic, financial, operational/resource and compliance exposures</p>\n<p>Explain causal interactions and common drivers</p>\n<p>Diagnose silo limitations</p>\n<p>Recommend holistic, integrated ERM with relevant risk responses</p>\n<p>Connect pre-loss planning, monitoring and value-based decisions</p>\n<p>Evaluate cost and implementation limitations</p>\n<p>These are suggested criteria. Exact mark allocations depend on the examiner's rubric.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 15 - Highest-priority topics for revision</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>I would prioritise the following based on the emphasis of your slides and how well the concepts support analytical examination questions.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Priority</th>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>What a strong answer should demonstrate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Evolution of RM</td>\n<td>Explain why each broadening of scope became necessary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Traditional CRM vs ERM</td>\n<td>Compare focus, scope, methods and value implications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Silo limitations</td>\n<td>Analyse common risk drivers and interconnected consequences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Strategic, tactical, operational and compliance risk</td>\n<td>Apply each category within a business scenario</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Pre-loss vs post-loss objectives</td>\n<td>Distinguish preparation from survival and recovery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Corporate goals and shareholder wealth</td>\n<td>Evaluate conflicting objectives and trade-offs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Legal forms of business</td>\n<td>Assess liability, ownership, control and continuity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medium-high</td>\n<td>RM process and aims</td>\n<td>Link systematic risk management to better decisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Holistic RM and emerging risks</td>\n<td>Explain sustainability, technological and regulatory developments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Role of risk managers</td>\n<td>Explain identifying, analysing and mitigating risks to objectives</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These priorities are study recommendations, not a claim about your lecturer's actual mark distribution.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2s-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 16 - Last-minute revision sheet</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Topic 2 essential relationships</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sole proprietorship</th>\n<th>Single ownership; unlimited owner liability</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>General partnership</td>\n<td>Shared business; potential joint and several liability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sdn. Bhd.</td>\n<td>Separate legal entity; generally limited shareholder liability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LLP</td>\n<td>Partnership flexibility with limited-liability features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strategic risk</td>\n<td>Does the organisation choose the right direction?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tactical risk</td>\n<td>How will the strategy be implemented?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operational risk</td>\n<td>Can daily activities be executed reliably?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compliance risk</td>\n<td>Are legal and regulatory requirements being met?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pre-loss objective</td>\n<td>Economical preparation, reduced anxiety, legal obligations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Post-loss objective</td>\n<td>Survival, continuity, earnings stability, recovery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Traditional RM</td>\n<td>Insurance and individual loss exposures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conventional CRM</td>\n<td>Credit, market and liquidity risks; hedging; often silos</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integrated RM</td>\n<td>Risk becomes part of planning and decision-making</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ERM</td>\n<td>Holistic enterprise-wide risk management supporting long-term value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Holistic RM</td>\n<td>Broader integration with sustainability, governance and business functions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>The single most important skill for Topic 2</h3>\n<p>A typical answer may identify:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"The company faces financial, operational and strategic risks.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>An excellent answer explains:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"The company's dependence on a single supplier creates resource exposure that could interrupt production. This may reduce revenue and intensify the funding pressure created by the expansion's debt financing. Because these exposures are managed by separate departments, management may underestimate the organisation's combined vulnerability. An integrated ERM approach would help evaluate these interactions before committing further capital, supporting better decisions about the expansion's long-term value.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That progression is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Identify → Explain → Connect → Evaluate → Justify\"><span>Identify</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Explain</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Connect</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluate</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Justify</span></div>\n<p>The greatest opportunity to earn higher-order marks in Topic 2 is usually in the last three stages.</p>\n<p>My suggested starting point is Question 5 in the practice examination: Evolution and Silo Weaknesses (12 marks). It connects the historical development, the limitations of traditional management and the purpose of ERM. If you can answer that convincingly without notes, you have a strong foundation for most of the chapter's longer subjective questions.</p></div></details></div></details>");
